  "summary": "Bristol Myers Squibb (BMS) is a multinational pharmaceutical company headquartered in Princeton, New Jersey. Its medicines treat cancer, heart conditions, autoimmune diseases, and blood disorders. In 2025, apixaban and nivolumab were its top products, and 69% of revenue came from the United States.\n\nBMS formed in 1989 through the merger of Bristol-Myers, founded in 1887, and Squibb, founded in 1858. Since 2007, it has shifted from a diversified drug company toward specialty biopharmaceuticals. Major acquisitions, including Celgene in 2019, expanded its drug portfolio and research pipeline. In 2024, the FDA approved Cobenfy, a new treatment for schizophrenia.\n\nThe company conducts research in the United States and several other countries. Its history also includes legal and accounting disputes. In the early 2000s, it paid settlements over allegations involving drug competition and improper sales practices.",
